BCC without 386 support: a dangling else drops the register of a long return.

In function.c the #ifdef I80386 block ended in "else", so when the compiler is
built without 386 support (as it must be to fit in 64K on XENIX) the following
block runs unconditionally and overwrites the storage just set for a long
return value. Code like n * fact(n - 1), where fact returns long, then pushes
only the low word. Moving the else outside the #ifdef restores the chain.

diff -ruN a/bcc/function.c b/bcc/function.c
--- a/bcc/function.c
+++ b/bcc/function.c
@@ -94,8 +94,8 @@
         else
 	    source->storage = RETURNREG;
     }
-    else
 #endif
+    else
     {
         if (source->type->scalar & DOUBLE)
 	    source->storage = doublreturnregs;
